Seeing no objections I=E2=80=99ll get this process underway.

spt

> On Nov 15, 2016, at 20:10, Sean Turner <sean@sn3rd.com> wrote:
>=20
> Note that Russ pointed out during the meeting that even though we can =
use this process a new RFC # will be minted at the end of the process.
>=20
> spt
>=20
>> On Nov 14, 2016, at 10:36, Sean Turner <sean@sn3rd.com> wrote:
>>=20
>> This email addresses the "Uplifting=E2=80=9D bullet on slide 6 of the =
chair slides =
(https://www.ietf.org/proceedings/97/slides/slides-97-tls-tls-wg-chair-sli=
des-00.pdf); this is entirely procedural (i.e., there=E2=80=99s really =
no technical ).
>>=20
>> The cipher suite registry's new "WG recommended=E2=80=9D column's =
=E2=80=9CY" values are being populated with cipher suites that are on =
standards track.  The notable exceptions are the EC-based AES-GCM =
ciphers defined in RFC 5289, which is an informational RFC.  This point =
is buried in an earlier version of draft-ietf-tls-tls13 and now in the =
soon to be draft-ietf-tls-iana-registry-updates (was =
draft-sandj-tls-iana-registry-updates); the complete list of the pet-TLS =
1.3 suites can be found here: =
https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-sandj-tls-iana-registry-updates-01#secti=
on-6.
>>=20
>> We can uplift RFC 5289 to PS from Informational with what essentially =
amounts to an IETF LC; we don't need a new draft (there's no errata).  =
We want to know if there are any objections to starting this process =
please post a message to the list by November 21st if you object (and =
why).
>>=20
>> Please note the following:
>>=20
>> -  This "action" is similar to what we're doing with 4492bis (it too =
is being moved to standards track) it's just that we can use this other =
process.
>>=20
>> - RFC 7525, which was published through the UTA WG and is a BCP btw, =
already 2119-RECOMMENDs the ciphers.
>>=20
>> - RFC 7540 (aka HTTP/2) MUSTs one of the RFC 5289 cipher suites.
>>=20
>> spt
>=20

